<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Vyatichi</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 1100 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The easternmost East Slavic tribe, settled on the upper Oka, and the last to give up its independence and its pagan gods.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyatichians">Vyatichi</a> descended from Vyatko, a leader who came from the west, and settled on the upper and middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oka-river">Oka</a>. Archaeology dates Slavic settlement there to the 8th century, where newcomers mingled with Baltic and Finnic inhabitants. Like their southern neighbours they paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hidden in dense forest far from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, they lived in small fortified settlements under their own chiefs and kept their clan customs. They traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, honey and wax along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oka-river">Oka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, and hoards of Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> are common in their lands. They clung to pagan burial rites well into the 12th century, and the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuksha">Kuksha</a> was killed among them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I</a> forced them to pay tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in the 960s, Vladimir I subdued them again in 981 and 982, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> campaigned against their chief Khodota in the late 11th century. Their lands were divided between the principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-principality-of-ryazan">Ryazan</a> and Rostov-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>, and later formed the heart of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vyatichi.jpg" alt="Vyatichi" width="320" height="164"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%B2%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8_%D0%A1%D0%B2%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D1%83_%D0%98%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D1%83.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 1100 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khodota</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Viatichi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=850&amp;focus=vyatichi" aria-label="Vyatichi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5602 219 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vyatichi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6131 811h0M5697 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5922 475h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5922 475h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=850&amp;focus=vyatichi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

